  "summary": "A movement in German art that emerged after World War I, characterized by a return to realism and a critical examination of society.",
  "description": "Characterized by precise, detailed, and unemotional depictions of everyday life, Neue Sachlichkeit rejected Expressionism's subjectivity in favor of objective observation. It emerged as a reaction to the emotional excesses of Expressionism and the trauma of World War I, often containing social commentary and criticism. The movement included both figurative and still-life works with a cool, detached perspective.",
  "culturalContext": "Neue Sachlichkeit — the \"New Objectivity\" G.F. Hartlaub named in 1923 — was Weimar Germany's cold-eyed return to realism: Otto Dix's war cripples and prostitutes, George Grosz's fat-cat caricatures, Christian Schad's clinical portraits, plus Verist and Magic-Realist wings. Rejecting Expressionist inwardness, it painted social fact with enamel clarity; its \"cold persona\" anticipated Photorealism and the entire post-conceptual return of figuration.",
